## Phase 2: Testing for Linear Flaws (The LCG Blind Test)
If the developer used a weak linear formula ($X_{n+1} = aX_n + c \pmod m$), the physical distances between consecutive spins will form a highly structured multi-dimensional pattern, even if you do not know $a$, $c$, or $m$.
## Step 1: Calculate Delta Vectors
Calculate the angular difference between consecutive spins:
$$\Delta \theta_n = (\theta_{n+1} - \theta_n) \pmod{360}$$ 
## Step 2: Spectral Test Plotting
Plot your data in multi-dimensional space. Take 3 consecutive differences and plot them as a coordinate point $(x, y, z)$ on a 3D graph:
$$\text{Point}_1 = (\Delta \theta_1, \Delta \theta_2, \Delta \theta_3)$$ 
$$\text{Point}_2 = (\Delta \theta_2, \Delta \theta_3, \Delta \theta_4)$$ 

* The Blind Reveal: If the generator is truly non-linear (secure), the points will look like a completely random cloud of dust. If the developer used a flawed linear generator, the points will instantly align into perfectly flat, parallel geometric hyperplanes (lines or sheets). This is a mathematical certainty known as Marsaglia's Theorem.

## Step 3: Lattice Solving
Once those lines appear on your plot, you have proven a linear vulnerability blindly. You can pass your angular data into an LLL (Lenstra-Lenstra-Lovász) algorithm script, which will automatically solve for the spacing of those hyperplanes, giving you the exact underlying multiplier ($a$) and modulus ($m$).
