## Phase 3: Testing for Time Flaws (The PRNG Re-Seed Test)
If the wheel passes the linear test, the developer might still be using a secure algorithm (like ChaCha20) but re-seeding it with a predictable value like system time every single spin.
## Step 1: The Idle Test
Let the game sit completely idle for exactly 10 minutes, then spin. Record the result. Do this multiple times.

* The Math: If the developer re-seeds with time, the vast gap in $t$ will cause a massive jump in the internal state.

## Step 2: The High-Speed Sequential Test
Trigger 10 spins as fast as the user interface allows.

* The Reveal: Look at the millisecond differences ($\Delta t = t_{n+1} - t_n$). If you find that spins executed within the same millisecond or exactly 1000ms apart yield identical or mathematically mirrored outcomes, the seed function is directly tied to the clock.

## Step 3: Polynomial Interpolation
If the output angle $\theta$ changes predictably relative to the execution time $t$, you can apply Lagrange Polynomial Interpolation to map the time-to-angle curve:
$$P(t) = \sum_{i=0}^{k} \theta_i \prod_{j \neq i} \frac{t - t_j}{t_i - t_j}$$ 
If $P(t)$ reliably yields a low-degree polynomial, you can blindly predict the winning angle simply by sending your spin request at a perfectly timed millisecond.
