## Phase 4: Testing for Modulo Bias (The Statistical Black-Box Test)
If the game is completely un-predictable spin-to-spin, you must check for structural imbalances caused by bad range-mapping math.
## Step 1: Large Sample Frequency Count
Collect a massive sample size (e.g., 10,000 blind spins). Count how many times the wheel lands on each individual slice.
## Step 2: Run a Chi-Square ($\chi^2$) Goodness-of-Fit Test
Set up your mathematical hypothesis test:

* Null Hypothesis ($H_0$): Every slice has an identical probability of hitting.
* Formula:
$$\chi^2 = \sum_{i=1}^{k} \frac{(O_i - E_i)^2}{E_i}$$ 
(Where $O_i$ is your observed count for a slice, and $E_i$ is the expected count, which is $\frac{\text{Total Spins}}{\text{Total Slices}}$).

## Step 3: Analyze the Critical Value
Look up your calculated $\chi^2$ value on a standard distribution chart. If your value exceeds the critical threshold (typically at a $p$-value $< 0.05$), the game is mathematically proven to be biased.
Because you are playing blindly, you do not need to know why it is biased. You simply shift all your actions to consistently back the specific physical slices that show a statistically significant higher win frequency, completely reversing the game's edge in your mathematical favor.
